Key What it says Example <code>command</code> the command line, as a person would type it <code>command: "show running-config"</code> <code>expect</code> <code>permit</code> or <code>deny</code>; required on a command test <code>expect: deny</code> <code>reason</code> the reason code the decision must carry, from the reason codes <code>reason: no_permit_dominates_deny</code> <p>The command is evaluated on the words the device sends for it. Where a platform splits an interface name from its index (IOS-XE), the test splits it the same way.</p> <p>What <code>reason:</code> adds. Without it, a test checks only the verdict. An <code>expect: deny</code> test passes whatever caused the deny, including a reason you did not mean: the subject lost its grant (<code>no_grants_matched</code>), or the device was quarantined. With <code>reason:</code>, the test also pins why. For example, this test passes only while a deny rule is what refuses the command:</p> <pre><code>tests:
  - {subject: netops, device: leaf1, command: "show running-config", expect: deny, reason: no_permit_dominates_deny}
</code></pre> <p><code>reason:</code> is checked on a permit test too (a permit carries <code>permit_dominates</code>). It must be a code from the closed vocabulary, or the file does not load.</p>
